1
00:00:00,131 --> 00:00:02,991
On Thursday, Nvidia lost more than four

2
00:00:03,011 --> 00:00:05,883
hundred billion dollars in a single morning,

3
00:00:05,903 --> 00:00:07,675
and it hadn't done anything wrong.

4
00:00:07,695 --> 00:00:08,545
Nobody had.

5
00:00:08,565 --> 00:00:09,665
No bank failed.

6
00:00:09,685 --> 00:00:11,189
No law passed.

7
00:00:11,209 --> 00:00:12,581
The economy looked fine.

8
00:00:12,601 --> 00:00:17,893
The S&P five hundred still lost more than two trillion dollars by midmorning.

9
00:00:17,913 --> 00:00:23,039
And the Nasdaq needed just six minutes to give back a rally that was one day old.

10
00:00:23,059 --> 00:00:25,871
So why did the stock market just crash?

11
00:00:25,891 --> 00:00:29,075
And was this the AI bubble popping?

12
00:00:29,095 --> 00:00:32,469
Best day after a midterm since nineteen eighty-two!

13
00:00:32,489 --> 00:00:33,826
Does this come with a receipt?

14
00:00:33,846 --> 00:00:38,730
The short answer is an election, six hundred thousand chips stuck in Hong Kong,

15
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:42,442
and some very rich people locking in a very good year.

16
00:00:42,462 --> 00:00:47,674
The long answer starts a month earlier, on the sixth of October, in Santa Clara,

17
00:00:47,694 --> 00:00:50,230
California.

18
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:54,562
That day, Nvidia closed at the highest price it had ever had.

19
00:00:54,582 --> 00:00:58,894
A month before, it was worth about five point four trillion dollars,

20
00:00:58,914 --> 00:01:03,192
roughly eight cents of every dollar in the whole S&P five hundred.

21
00:01:03,212 --> 00:01:08,364
If you own a plain index fund, the safe, boring kind everyone recommends,

22
00:01:08,384 --> 00:01:10,396
you own a lot of Nvidia.

23
00:01:10,416 --> 00:01:15,528
Add the rest of the Magnificent Seven, Apple, Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet,

24
00:01:15,548 --> 00:01:16,860
Meta and Tesla,

25
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:19,020
and about thirty-five dollars of every

26
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,270
hundred in that fund sits on seven companies.

27
00:01:22,290 --> 00:01:23,462
I'm diversified.

28
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:25,242
Five hundred companies.

29
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:28,304
Seven of them are a third of the pie.

30
00:01:28,324 --> 00:01:30,542
...That is a big slice.

31
00:01:30,562 --> 00:01:34,694
Even at the dot-com peak, the market wasn't this lopsided.

32
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:37,778
And most of those giants are betting on the same thing.

33
00:01:37,798 --> 00:01:39,790
That bet is expensive.

34
00:01:39,810 --> 00:01:40,590
This year,

35
00:01:40,610 --> 00:01:45,550
the four biggest cloud companies plan to spend over seven hundred billion dollars

36
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:46,902
building data centers.

37
00:01:46,922 --> 00:01:49,086
Next year, more than a trillion.

38
00:01:49,106 --> 00:01:52,098
The biggest line on that shopping list says Nvidia,

39
00:01:52,118 --> 00:01:55,530
whose last quarter brought in ninety-six billion dollars,

40
00:01:55,550 --> 00:01:59,243
more than double the year before.

41
00:01:59,263 --> 00:02:03,422
By late October, investors were staring at four cracks.

42
00:02:03,442 --> 00:02:06,146
Crack one: the money goes in a circle.

43
00:02:06,166 --> 00:02:08,758
Nvidia invests in its own customers,

44
00:02:08,778 --> 00:02:12,950
the AI labs and chip-rental firms that then buy Nvidia chips.

45
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:16,482
It owns a stake in CoreWeave, which rents those chips out,

46
00:02:16,502 --> 00:02:21,202
and agreed to buy up to six point three billion dollars of the computing power

47
00:02:21,222 --> 00:02:22,554
CoreWeave can't sell.

48
00:02:22,574 --> 00:02:27,546
Nvidia's own finance chief knows how that sounds: "we know some will call this

49
00:02:27,566 --> 00:02:29,069
circular financing."

50
00:02:29,089 --> 00:02:31,415
Here's a few billion dollars.

51
00:02:31,435 --> 00:02:32,227
Wow!

52
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:34,277
What should I spend it on?

53
00:02:34,297 --> 00:02:35,519
Funny you should ask.

54
00:02:35,539 --> 00:02:38,223
Crack two: the chips are the collateral.

55
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:42,867
Firms like CoreWeave borrowed huge sums to buy chips, rented them out,

56
00:02:42,887 --> 00:02:45,479
and pledged those same chips to borrow more.

57
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:50,411
At one point CoreWeave owed over five dollars for every dollar of equity.

58
00:02:50,431 --> 00:02:53,103
Fine, while a used chip holds its value.

59
00:02:53,123 --> 00:02:56,635
But Nvidia brings out a new generation every year or two,

60
00:02:56,655 --> 00:02:59,567
and each one makes the last one worth less.

61
00:02:59,587 --> 00:03:02,219
And Michael Burry, who called the housing crash,

62
00:03:02,239 --> 00:03:06,131
says the giants book their servers as lasting longer than they do,

63
00:03:06,151 --> 00:03:07,552
which flatters profits.

64
00:03:07,572 --> 00:03:10,144
The nightmare: one borrower misses one

65
00:03:10,164 --> 00:03:12,888
payment, the lender grabs the chips to sell,

66
00:03:12,908 --> 00:03:17,480
and everyone learns that afternoon what a used AI chip is worth in a hurry.

67
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:18,632
It hasn't happened.

68
00:03:18,652 --> 00:03:21,167
But every chip investor has thought about it.

69
00:03:21,187 --> 00:03:23,151
Crack three: who's paying.

70
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:24,443
By the end of July,

71
00:03:24,463 --> 00:03:29,563
Nvidia was owed about sixty-three billion dollars for chips it had shipped but not

72
00:03:29,583 --> 00:03:30,755
been paid for yet.

73
00:03:30,775 --> 00:03:35,519
And this year, just three buyers made up more than two fifths of its sales.

74
00:03:35,539 --> 00:03:40,279
Picture a restaurant where three tables order two of every five plates,

75
00:03:40,299 --> 00:03:42,293
and a lot of the food goes on a tab.

76
00:03:42,313 --> 00:03:44,037
Crack four: the plug.

77
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:45,309
You can buy a chip.

78
00:03:45,329 --> 00:03:48,061
You can't buy electricity that doesn't exist.

79
00:03:48,081 --> 00:03:51,593
In northern Virginia, the data-center capital of the world,

80
00:03:51,613 --> 00:03:56,456
the wait to hook a new building up to the grid had stretched to about seven years.

81
00:03:56,476 --> 00:03:59,800
And the power that does exist, somebody pays for.

82
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:03,352
On America's biggest grid, across thirteen states,

83
00:04:03,372 --> 00:04:08,436
power plants get paid just to stand ready, and families cover that in their bills.

84
00:04:08,456 --> 00:04:10,488
Then the data centers piled in.

85
00:04:10,508 --> 00:04:15,048
Households there were paying an estimated twenty-five to thirty dollars a month more

86
00:04:15,068 --> 00:04:21,258
than in twenty twenty-four, and the grid's own watchdog blamed data centers first.

87
00:04:21,278 --> 00:04:23,443
Why is the electric bill up again?

88
00:04:23,463 --> 00:04:24,808
Hi, neighbor!

89
00:04:24,828 --> 00:04:27,060
Nobody reads a spending report.

90
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,872
Everybody reads their power bill.

91
00:04:28,892 --> 00:04:29,753
Hold that thought.

92
00:04:29,773 --> 00:04:31,985
Wall Street had its own bad news.

93
00:04:32,005 --> 00:04:36,237
On October twenty-ninth, Google had its worst trading day ever.

94
00:04:36,257 --> 00:04:41,121
The next day Intel fell by a quarter, chip stocks slid into a bear market,

95
00:04:41,141 --> 00:04:44,718
and Apple, the boring one, passed Nvidia in value.

96
00:04:44,738 --> 00:04:46,398
By the second of November,

97
00:04:46,418 --> 00:04:51,638
the forty-seven biggest AI and chip stocks had lost about five trillion dollars

98
00:04:51,658 --> 00:04:52,430
since the top.

99
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:55,282
That's about what the whole dot-com crash wiped out,

100
00:04:55,302 --> 00:04:57,594
and that took two and a half years.

101
00:04:57,614 --> 00:04:59,686
This took four weeks.

102
00:04:59,706 --> 00:05:02,879
Then came the election.

103
00:05:02,899 --> 00:05:05,511
Tuesday, the third of November.

104
00:05:05,531 --> 00:05:07,023
Democrats won the House.

105
00:05:07,043 --> 00:05:08,264
Then they won the Senate.

106
00:05:08,284 --> 00:05:10,904
Further down the ballot, in ten states,

107
00:05:10,924 --> 00:05:14,036
there were forty-one local votes on data centers.

108
00:05:14,056 --> 00:05:16,248
Voters went against them in forty.

109
00:05:16,268 --> 00:05:18,360
Twenty-one of those were in Ohio.

110
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:21,906
The one town that said yes was Harwood, North Dakota.

111
00:05:21,926 --> 00:05:23,778
Ohio, no.

112
00:05:23,798 --> 00:05:25,330
Ohio, no.

113
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:26,902
Ohio, no.

114
00:05:26,922 --> 00:05:27,752
Ohio...

115
00:05:27,772 --> 00:05:28,472
Hi!

116
00:05:28,492 --> 00:05:29,192
Yes!

117
00:05:29,212 --> 00:05:30,037
Over here!

118
00:05:30,057 --> 00:05:32,921
That's the power bill, showing up at the ballot box.

119
00:05:32,941 --> 00:05:36,081
In Ohio, Sherrod Brown beat Jon Husted,

120
00:05:36,101 --> 00:05:40,833
who'd been called "the face of data centers." In Texas, Elon Musk,

121
00:05:40,853 --> 00:05:44,873
the cycle's biggest donor with at least four hundred and seventy-four million

122
00:05:44,893 --> 00:05:47,337
dollars, lost both big races.

123
00:05:47,357 --> 00:05:48,606
His reaction:

124
00:05:48,626 --> 00:05:52,185
Data centers in space looking pretty appealing right now.

125
00:05:52,205 --> 00:05:54,957
Here's why a town vote moves Wall Street.

126
00:05:54,977 --> 00:05:59,993
A data center needs land, power and a permit, and all three are local.

127
00:06:00,013 --> 00:06:02,233
Goldman Sachs figured moratoriums like

128
00:06:02,253 --> 00:06:04,993
these could hit about forty-five percent of

129
00:06:05,013 --> 00:06:07,310
America's planned data-center expansion.

130
00:06:07,330 --> 00:06:09,642
And the bigger fear was Washington.

131
00:06:09,662 --> 00:06:13,154
For years, AI was mostly left alone to build.

132
00:06:13,174 --> 00:06:18,626
Now a Democratic Congress, elected the same night voters said no to data centers,

133
00:06:18,646 --> 00:06:22,570
might want rules on where data centers go, who pays for their power,

134
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:24,622
and how fast the build-out runs.

135
00:06:24,642 --> 00:06:32,680
One congressman, Robert Garcia, had two words for AI executives: "Lawyer up."

136
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:33,431
So.

137
00:06:33,451 --> 00:06:35,587
Data centers.

138
00:06:35,607 --> 00:06:36,824
We were just leaving.

139
00:06:36,844 --> 00:06:38,496
No rule has passed.

140
00:06:38,516 --> 00:06:39,408
Not one.

141
00:06:39,428 --> 00:06:41,900
It was the fear of a rule that did the selling.

142
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:46,652
That trillion-dollar shopping list had picked up some small print: if they let us

143
00:06:46,672 --> 00:06:49,093
build it.

144
00:06:49,113 --> 00:06:52,097
And on Wednesday, Wall Street partied anyway.

145
00:06:52,117 --> 00:06:56,889
The Dow jumped fifteen hundred and sixty-nine points, about three percent,

146
00:06:56,909 --> 00:07:00,541
its best day after a midterm since nineteen eighty-two,

147
00:07:00,561 --> 00:07:02,853
and oil fell more than nine percent.

148
00:07:02,873 --> 00:07:04,825
Traders loved the cheap oil.

149
00:07:04,845 --> 00:07:06,737
They hadn't gotten to the new Congress yet.

150
00:07:06,757 --> 00:07:08,610
The party lasted one day.

151
00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:13,450
That morning, Vice President JD Vance had posted on X.

152
00:07:13,470 --> 00:07:15,552
The reason we lost is the war.

153
00:07:15,572 --> 00:07:17,104
The war with Iran.

154
00:07:17,124 --> 00:07:20,056
That raised hopes of a deal, and oil fell.

155
00:07:20,076 --> 00:07:24,013
By Thursday morning, traders doubted it, and oil climbed again.

156
00:07:24,033 --> 00:07:28,277
Then, before the opening bell, Reuters dropped the next problem.

157
00:07:28,297 --> 00:07:33,549
About six hundred thousand of Nvidia's H200 chips were sitting in Hong Kong,

158
00:07:33,569 --> 00:07:35,566
waiting for Beijing to let them in.

159
00:07:35,586 --> 00:07:39,338
Washington had cleared the sales last winter, case by case,

160
00:07:39,358 --> 00:07:41,290
with a twenty-five percent tariff.

161
00:07:41,310 --> 00:07:44,554
In August, Beijing let companies use them in Hong Kong.

162
00:07:44,574 --> 00:07:46,826
Into mainland China, in bulk?

163
00:07:46,846 --> 00:07:47,889
Still waiting.

164
00:07:47,909 --> 00:07:50,063
You said we can sell them!

165
00:07:50,083 --> 00:07:50,895
You can.

166
00:07:50,915 --> 00:07:52,680
Twenty-five percent, please.

167
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:53,792
They're in Hong Kong!

168
00:07:53,812 --> 00:07:56,486
Can they come in?

169
00:07:56,506 --> 00:07:57,399
Not yet.

170
00:07:57,419 --> 00:08:02,471
The strange part: Nvidia had already taken a four hundred million dollar hit on

171
00:08:02,491 --> 00:08:04,703
H200s that nobody bought.

172
00:08:04,723 --> 00:08:09,673
And the ones that did have buyers couldn't get through the door.

173
00:08:09,693 --> 00:08:10,645
Nine thirty.

174
00:08:10,665 --> 00:08:12,423
The bell rings in New York.

175
00:08:12,443 --> 00:08:16,455
It takes the Nasdaq six minutes to give back the whole rally.

176
00:08:16,475 --> 00:08:17,707
So much for Wednesday.

177
00:08:17,727 --> 00:08:18,919
Ten thirty-five.

178
00:08:18,939 --> 00:08:23,251
The Nasdaq is down four percent and the Dow seven hundred points.

179
00:08:23,271 --> 00:08:27,903
Across the S&P five hundred, more than two trillion dollars has vanished,

180
00:08:27,923 --> 00:08:31,315
and more than four hundred billion of it is Nvidia alone.

181
00:08:31,335 --> 00:08:35,263
That's about twice Amazon's entire building budget for the year.

182
00:08:35,283 --> 00:08:36,624
Eleven seventeen.

183
00:08:36,644 --> 00:08:41,327
The VIX, Wall Street's fear gauge, is up sixty-five percent.

184
00:08:41,347 --> 00:08:45,350
Think of it as the price of crash insurance, and everybody wants some.

185
00:08:45,370 --> 00:08:46,842
Eleven thirty-seven.

186
00:08:46,862 --> 00:08:52,242
The Dow is on track for its worst second day after a midterm in records going back

187
00:08:52,262 --> 00:08:53,936
to eighteen ninety-six.

188
00:08:53,956 --> 00:08:55,068
One thirty-eight.

189
00:08:55,088 --> 00:09:00,554
AMD's boss, Lisa Su, speaks up, with her stock down fifteen percent.

190
00:09:00,574 --> 00:09:05,230
I think the market can sometimes be a little bit short-sighted.

191
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:06,392
Aaaaah!

192
00:09:06,412 --> 00:09:07,444
Two fifty-eight.

193
00:09:07,464 --> 00:09:10,060
And this is where ordinary people come in.

194
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:13,093
Maybe you're at work, checking your phone under the desk.

195
00:09:13,113 --> 00:09:15,964
You own a plain S&P five hundred fund.

196
00:09:15,984 --> 00:09:18,376
If it holds a quarter of a million dollars,

197
00:09:18,396 --> 00:09:23,028
Nvidia alone took about nine hundred and ninety dollars out of it that morning.

198
00:09:23,048 --> 00:09:26,260
By now, small investors have sold, on net,

199
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:29,492
three hundred and fifty-eight million dollars of stock,

200
00:09:29,512 --> 00:09:31,678
the most since March twenty twenty.

201
00:09:31,698 --> 00:09:34,415
Buy the dip...

202
00:09:34,435 --> 00:09:35,795
...or sell the dip?

203
00:09:35,815 --> 00:09:41,899
Viraj Patel at Vanda Research called it "a total capitulation by retail investors,"

204
00:09:41,919 --> 00:09:44,138
with "no signs of buying the dip."

205
00:09:44,158 --> 00:09:45,310
Three oh six.

206
00:09:45,330 --> 00:09:50,406
Charlie McElligott, a strategist at Nomura, explains who's really selling.

207
00:09:50,426 --> 00:09:51,981
They're shooting the generals.

208
00:09:52,001 --> 00:09:54,753
The generals are the year's biggest winners.

209
00:09:54,773 --> 00:09:59,337
Hedge funds had made a fortune in twenty twenty-six, and with two months to go,

210
00:09:59,357 --> 00:10:01,189
they sold the winners to lock it in.

211
00:10:01,209 --> 00:10:05,621
Computer-driven funds and funds on borrowed money were forced to sell too.

212
00:10:05,641 --> 00:10:07,789
And nobody stepped in to buy.

213
00:10:07,809 --> 00:10:08,701
Great year.

214
00:10:08,721 --> 00:10:09,613
Let's bank it.

215
00:10:09,633 --> 00:10:11,079
Sell the winners.

216
00:10:11,099 --> 00:10:13,058
Which ones?

217
00:10:13,078 --> 00:10:13,830
Yes.

218
00:10:13,850 --> 00:10:17,346
Single-name stock moves are absolutely BONKERS.

219
00:10:17,366 --> 00:10:18,458
Four o'clock.

220
00:10:18,478 --> 00:10:19,628
The closing bell.

221
00:10:19,648 --> 00:10:23,180
The Dow lost eighteen hundred and thirty-three points.

222
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:25,672
The S&P fell over four percent.

223
00:10:25,692 --> 00:10:30,364
The Nasdaq fell five and a half, its biggest drop in nineteen months,

224
00:10:30,384 --> 00:10:34,348
and is now in a correction, at least ten percent below its high.

225
00:10:34,368 --> 00:10:37,640
Chip stocks had their worst day since March twenty twenty.

226
00:10:37,660 --> 00:10:42,404
Nvidia closed at a hundred and eighty-one dollars, down more than nine percent.

227
00:10:42,424 --> 00:10:46,788
And while everything burned, AppLovin rose almost thirty percent,

228
00:10:46,808 --> 00:10:51,160
and Goldman Sachs picked that day to name a hundred and nine new partners,

229
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:55,389
its biggest class since twenty ten.

230
00:10:55,409 --> 00:10:56,181
Honestly?

231
00:10:56,201 --> 00:10:59,662
Great day to get promoted.

232
00:10:59,682 --> 00:11:03,394
It's easy to think a crash like this only hurts rich people.

233
00:11:03,414 --> 00:11:04,110
It doesn't.

234
00:11:04,130 --> 00:11:08,022
Around seventy million Americans save in a 401(k),

235
00:11:08,042 --> 00:11:09,914
mostly through the same index funds,

236
00:11:09,934 --> 00:11:13,066
and the pensions of teachers and firefighters hold them too.

237
00:11:13,086 --> 00:11:15,745
When the seven giants fall, they fall too.

238
00:11:15,765 --> 00:11:17,697
It goes deeper than stocks.

239
00:11:17,717 --> 00:11:20,089
Data centers run on borrowed money,

240
00:11:20,109 --> 00:11:22,961
much of it raised from insurers and pension funds.

241
00:11:22,981 --> 00:11:27,601
Pimco bought a big piece of the bonds for Meta's giant Louisiana data center with

242
00:11:27,621 --> 00:11:32,705
savers' money, and Washington has moved to open 401(k)s to this kind of lending.

243
00:11:32,725 --> 00:11:37,166
So your retirement may be quietly lending to a half-built building.

244
00:11:37,186 --> 00:11:40,770
And some get hit without owning a share: the electricians,

245
00:11:40,790 --> 00:11:45,582
some making two hundred and sixty thousand dollars a year on data-center jobs,

246
00:11:45,602 --> 00:11:47,394
and the diners that feed them.

247
00:11:47,414 --> 00:11:49,326
Thursday didn't stop that work.

248
00:11:49,346 --> 00:11:52,581
But if the spending stops, it lands there first.

249
00:11:52,601 --> 00:11:55,573
If you're twenty-five, Thursday was a sale.

250
00:11:55,593 --> 00:12:02,222
If you're sixty-two and retiring in the spring, it was a very long day.

251
00:12:02,242 --> 00:12:04,234
So who do you get angry at?

252
00:12:04,254 --> 00:12:06,126
Every crash wants a villain.

253
00:12:06,146 --> 00:12:07,858
Twenty oh eight had the banks.

254
00:12:07,878 --> 00:12:10,158
Thursday doesn't really have one.

255
00:12:10,178 --> 00:12:12,244
I just didn't want the bill.

256
00:12:12,264 --> 00:12:14,538
I just wanted to keep my good year.

257
00:12:14,558 --> 00:12:16,653
I just said not yet.

258
00:12:16,673 --> 00:12:18,417
I just sold chips!

259
00:12:18,437 --> 00:12:21,289
Every one of those choices made sense on its own.

260
00:12:21,309 --> 00:12:23,598
They just all landed on the same morning.

261
00:12:23,618 --> 00:12:26,210
So here's why the stock market crashed.

262
00:12:26,230 --> 00:12:30,142
A market that's one-third AI had been sliding for a month.

263
00:12:30,162 --> 00:12:35,466
Then, in three days, voters elected a Congress that might rein in data centers,

264
00:12:35,486 --> 00:12:38,698
Beijing left six hundred thousand chips at the door,

265
00:12:38,718 --> 00:12:41,710
and funds sitting on a great year cashed out.

266
00:12:41,730 --> 00:12:42,702
Nothing broke.

267
00:12:42,722 --> 00:12:43,818
The fear did it.

268
00:12:43,838 --> 00:12:45,390
It isn't new, either.

269
00:12:45,410 --> 00:12:49,142
In the eighteen-forties, Britain went mad for railways,

270
00:12:49,162 --> 00:12:53,654
and at the peak it was pouring about seven percent of its economy into track.

271
00:12:53,674 --> 00:12:56,946
Railway shares lost nearly two thirds of their value,

272
00:12:56,966 --> 00:13:00,298
and a third or more of the approved lines never got built.

273
00:13:00,318 --> 00:13:04,759
But many of the lines that did get built are still part of Britain's railway today.

274
00:13:04,779 --> 00:13:05,951
Here's the catch.

275
00:13:05,971 --> 00:13:08,363
A railway can wait for its trains.

276
00:13:08,383 --> 00:13:11,456
A chip sitting in a dark building just gets older.

277
00:13:11,476 --> 00:13:14,691
So, was Thursday the AI bubble popping?

278
00:13:14,711 --> 00:13:15,703
Not yet.

279
00:13:15,723 --> 00:13:19,575
Both tariff-crash days in April twenty twenty-five were bigger,

280
00:13:19,595 --> 00:13:23,987
and together erased a record six point six trillion dollars.

281
00:13:24,007 --> 00:13:27,879
Jobless claims were near their lowest since nineteen sixty-nine.

282
00:13:27,899 --> 00:13:29,771
And no rule had passed.

283
00:13:29,791 --> 00:13:33,954
Bernie Sanders called for a national moratorium on data centers.

284
00:13:33,974 --> 00:13:37,993
Americans don't want Big Tech oligarchs wrecking the environment...

285
00:13:38,013 --> 00:13:39,887
A call, not a bill.

286
00:13:39,907 --> 00:13:42,599
A bubble doesn't pop when prices fall.

287
00:13:42,619 --> 00:13:44,591
It pops when the money stops.

288
00:13:44,611 --> 00:13:47,303
On Thursday, the money hadn't stopped.

289
00:13:47,323 --> 00:13:49,575
Nvidia's sales were still doubling.

290
00:13:49,595 --> 00:13:53,949
The giants were still expected to spend more than a trillion dollars next year.

291
00:13:53,969 --> 00:13:56,741
But here's the part almost nobody noticed.

292
00:13:56,761 --> 00:14:00,105
A week before the crash, one giant blinked.

293
00:14:00,125 --> 00:14:02,265
Microsoft cut its spending plan from a

294
00:14:02,285 --> 00:14:04,865
hundred and seventy-five billion dollars to a

295
00:14:04,885 --> 00:14:06,037
hundred and seventy.

296
00:14:06,057 --> 00:14:07,949
That's less than three percent.

297
00:14:07,969 --> 00:14:08,601
Tiny.

298
00:14:08,621 --> 00:14:11,133
But it's a foot moving toward the brake.

299
00:14:11,153 --> 00:14:12,253
And on Tuesday,

300
00:14:12,273 --> 00:14:13,933
the people who pay the electric bill

301
00:14:13,953 --> 00:14:16,413
elected a Congress that might want a say in

302
00:14:16,433 --> 00:14:17,627
where the buildings go.

303
00:14:17,647 --> 00:14:19,679
So Thursday wasn't the pop.

304
00:14:19,699 --> 00:14:23,451
It was the day the market noticed there's a pin, and that voters,

305
00:14:23,471 --> 00:14:26,681
Washington and Beijing all have a hand on it.

306
00:14:26,701 --> 00:14:30,413
Nvidia is now about a quarter below its October record.

307
00:14:30,433 --> 00:14:33,425
Its next report is due on the seventeenth of November,

308
00:14:33,445 --> 00:14:36,777
and the six hundred thousand chips are still in Hong Kong.

309
00:14:36,797 --> 00:14:39,209
The rally died in six minutes.

310
00:14:39,229 --> 00:14:44,533
What happens next gets decided much more slowly, by permits, power lines,

311
00:14:44,553 --> 00:14:47,537
and an electric bill on a kitchen table in Ohio.

312
00:14:47,557 --> 00:14:52,449
On Thursday, Lisa Su said the market can be a little bit short-sighted.

313
00:14:52,469 --> 00:14:57,459
That afternoon, it might have been the furthest it had looked in months.

